\par
{\b\fs28 **2.3.2 Why These 7 Constraints Are Universal**}\par

\par
{\b The 7 constraints are not domain-specific. They emerge from gradient resolution itself.}\par
\par
When you have a system that:\par
1. Uses gradient resolution to evolve\par
2. Has bounded propagation speed\par
3. Requires distinguishable states\par
4. Is subject to noise\par
5. Has sampling limitations\par
6. Allows self-reference\par
7. Has communication costs\par
\par
{\b Then these 7 constraints MUST appear in your domain.}\par
\par
{\b Proof by cross-domain mapping:}\par
\par
{\b Constraint 1: Two-State Limitation} appears identically in all domains. Physics has electron spin (up/down). Computing has bits (1/0). Biology has cells (alive/dead). Psychology has consciousness states (aware/unaware). Economics has transactions (complete/fail). All 17 domains have binary states because all have coherent systems that require distinguishable states.\par
\par
{\b Constraint 2: Sequential Processing Bottleneck} appears in causality across all domains. Physics enforces cause before effect. Computing requires fetch→decode→execute sequences. Biology needs transcription before translation. Learning requires sequential training epochs. Social systems transmit culture generation-to-generation. All 17 domains show that causality creates sequential bottlenecks.\par
\par
{\b Constraint 3: Size-Speed Tradeoff} appears in proximity costs across all domains. Physics: inner orbits are fast, outer orbits are slow. Computing: registers are fast/small, disk is slow/large. Biology: local synapses are fast, hormones are slow. Neuroscience: working memory is local/fast, long-term memory is distributed/slow. Networks: local clusters are fast, global systems are slow. All 17 domains show that proximity is required for speed.\par
\par
{\b Constraint 4: Error Rate Fundamental} appears in noise across all domains. Physics has measurement uncertainty and thermal fluctuations. Computing has bit flip errors. Biology has mutation rates. Medicine has drug side effects. Economics has market surprises. All 17 domains show that perfect isolation is impossible and error rates > 0.\par
\par
{\b Constraint 5: Complexity Scaling Exponentially} appears in hard problems across all domains. Physics: 2-body solvable, N-body exponential. Computing: sorting is O(n log n), traveling salesman is O(n!). Biology: single genes predictable, gene networks exponential. Machine learning: simple models efficient, neural network landscapes exponential. All 17 domains show NP-complete problems that cannot be solved faster.\par
\par
{\b Constraint 6: Undecidability} appears in logical limits across all domains. Physics: complementary variables cannot both be known. Computing: halting problem is undecidable. Philosophy: Gödel incompleteness means some truths are unprovable. Psychology: consciousness is subjective/unmeasurable. All 17 domains show that self-reference creates logical limits.\par
\par
{\b Constraint 7: Communication Bottleneck} appears in bandwidth limits across all domains. Physics: light cone limits causality. Computing: CPU much faster than memory, memory faster than network. Biology: local neural transmission is fast, hormonal signals slow. Economics: local trading immediate, international trade delayed. All 17 domains show that processing speed exceeds retrieval speed.\par
\par
{\b Every domain has all 7 constraints.}\par
\par
This is not coincidence. {\b This is proof that UPFM is correct}: if the model were domain-specific, we'd see different constraints in different domains. But we see the same 7 everywhere.\par
\par
