{\b\fs28 **2.3.3 Design Implications: How to Work WITH Constraints**}\par

\par
{\b Proper approach}:\par
\par
1. {\b Identify} which constraints apply to your system\par
2. {\b Understand} their root reasons (gradient resolution principles)\par
3. {\b Optimize} within the constraints (not against them)\par
4. {\b Plan} for the constraint costs\par
\par
{\b Example: Building a High-Speed Computer System}\par
\par
Constraint 3 (Size-Speed): You cannot have a large fast system.\par
\par
{\b Improper approach}: \par
\pard\li720 ❌ "We'll just design it faster" (ignores physics)\par\pard
\pard\li720 ❌ "We'll put the data closer" (too late, data exists distributed)\par\pard
\pard\li720 ❌ "We'll use faster CPUs" (helps, but bandwidth still limited)\par\pard
\par
{\b Proper approach}:\par
\pard\li720 ✅ Accept the constraint: cache hierarchy is necessary\par\pard
\pard\li720 ✅ Optimize within it: L1 (small/fast), L2 (medium/medium), L3 (large/slow), RAM (larger/slower), disk (huge/slowest)\par\pard
\pard\li720 ✅ Plan data locality: move computation to data, not data to computation\par\pard
\pard\li720 ✅ Understand the cost: complexity of cache coherence is the price of having multiple layers\par\pard
\par
{\b Result}: Modern CPUs achieve 3+ GHz by accepting and optimizing within the constraint, not fighting it.\par
